## 5. Global and market cross-check

{{ c("C-H3-03") }} A stronger renminbi alongside higher US yields gives the PBOC some room. A stronger currency also dampens imported inflation, which matters if the price turn depends on imported costs. The China 10-year government bond yield is the market's own read of nominal growth. It has no redistributable public source and is therefore not part of this public build.

## 6. Judgment

{{ c("I-05") }} The demand leg is the weak link. At the baseline, fewer demand tests passed than confirmation requires, and those that passed did so on marginal changes from weak levels. We therefore put the analyst probability that the thesis is confirmed at **{{ state.probability_pct }}**, with **{{ state.confidence_label|lower }}** confidence. This is a judgment, not a model output. Confidence is low for three reasons: GDP history is a revised vintage rather than first prints, consensus forecasts are not available in the public build, and both oil and exports can swing sharply.

## 7. What would change our mind

| Release | What we will check | Moves |
|---|---|---|
| September CPI and PPI | PPI momentum, PPI versus six months ago, core CPI, consumer-goods PPI | H1, H2, H0 |
| Third-quarter GDP and September activity | implied deflator for 2026Q3, retail sales | H1, H2 |
| September credit data | household mortgage flow, M1, credit growth | H2, H3 |

**Toward confirmation:** core CPI turning up, consumer-goods PPI turning positive (pass-through), net mortgage lending turning positive and, on policy, incremental measures showing up as faster government-bond financing and budget spending.

**Toward falsification:** PPI momentum and demand failing together (the early falsification rule), or the deflator slipping back below zero once oil base effects fade.

## 8. Research implications (conditional)

These are conditional research implications of the macro scenarios, not recommendations and not forecasts of market levels. The system tests the macro scenarios against preregistered rules; it does not test the market implications, which follow from them.

